@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — a gate that hands over a token states no failure; a gate
# that asks a question states what a wrong answer costs
#
# One generalization, formed on 2026-09-27 out of the reading of the second
# book of Jeu and four nodes that were already in the holding. It is the
# survivor of a counter-search that killed the candidate this run set out with:
# that a transmission teaching many stations always also carries one item
# opening all of them. Two of the five ascent traditions in the holding refuted
# that at once, and the refuting cases turned out to be witnesses for the
# division below. The search is at examinations/searches-books-of-jeu.ttl.
#
# The statement belongs beside pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls rather than
# under it. That one asks what a transmission says about an outcome it cannot
# fix; this one asks what it says about a passage it entirely controls, and the
# answer runs the other way: where the transmission writes both the question
# and the answer, it stops writing as soon as the answer could be wrong.
#

pmc:ThePassageThatPutsNoQuestion a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Where the gate hands over the token, no transmission states a failure at it"@en, "Wo das Tor den Ausweis übergibt, nennt keine Überlieferung ein Misslingen an ihm"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission carries a graded passage in which the passer is admitted at each station by producing a token that the station itself or the transmission supplies him — a seal, a name, a number, a garment — it states no test at any station and names no case in which a passer failed to be admitted; where instead a station puts a question to the passer or subjects him to an ordeal, the same transmission states in the operative passage what failing it costs."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:JeuCustodesTacent, pmc:JeuThesaurusTransitusForma, pmc:PistisSophiaTransitusVisu, pmc:PistisSophiaVestisNominum, pmc:WaterQuestionCaution, pmc:MithraicProbatioLetalis ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "The four witnesses of the first limb, and what each of them supplies. In the Books of Jeu the watchers of the gates of the light-treasure read a seal off the forehead of the passer and a cipher out of his hands, open, and expressly do not speak with him; each rank beyond them then gives him its seal, its mystery and the great name, so that the token of the next station is issued at the last."@en ;
    skos:note "The treasury passages of the first book of Jeu give the same arrangement one level down: the passer is sealed with the seal of the station, the father of the station gives him his own name and seal, and the watchers of the next gate scatter because they have recognised their father's seal. In the Pistis Sophia the gates open of themselves, the rulers look at the light-garment, see on it the mystery that holds their own names, and their bonds loose without a word being exchanged; and the garment itself was put on before the ascent began and carries the name of every mystery and every order below. None of the four states a test, and none names a case in which a passer was refused."@en ;
    skos:note "What the two witnesses of the second limb do instead. The Hekhalot descent puts one question at the sixth palace, about the nature of the water, and the whole penalty of the corpus hangs on it — and on the question and not on a deed: what the descender must not do there is ask, and the transmission attaches the consequence to that speech act. The Mithraic report has graded ordeals of fire, cold, hunger and thirst, and states both outcomes in one sentence: the highest initiation to the candidate still alive at the end, an honourable burial to the one who has succumbed. Both stand in the operative or reporting passage and not in a later gloss."@en ;
    skos:note "Why the division is worth stating rather than the observation that some gates test and some do not. What the two limbs together claim is a relation between two features of the same transmission: whether the passer supplies anything out of himself, and whether the transmission admits that the passage can fail. A handbook that hands over every token has written the answer to its own question, and it then has nothing to say about a wrong answer — not out of carelessness but because in its own terms there is no such case. That is falsifiable in one direction by a token-handing passage that states a failure, and in the other by an ordeal or a question stated without a cost."@en ;
    skos:note "The case that bounds the statement and does not break it, and it is the reason the first limb says supplied rather than possessed. The Egyptian gate material of this holding has the doorway ask the dead man its name, and the names are printed on the same sheet as the question; the consequence of not answering is refusal of passage and is stated. Read as a question it is a witness for the second limb; read as a token supplied by the book it is a counter-case to the first. The statement takes it as the second, on the ground that the wording is a question put to the passer, and it says so here rather than leaving the decision unmarked. Anybody who reads it the other way has a refutation of the first limb and needs nothing from this graph to bring it."@en ;
    skos:note "Counter-search, carried against the compiled holding before the statement was written, as SPEC 16 requires, and it changed what was written. The search was set up for a different candidate — that a many-stationed passage always carries a single master item — and the Mithraic ladder of seven gates and the Hekhalot palaces refuted it, the first carrying no such item and the second issuing seals palace by palace. Both then read as witnesses for the division above. The same search found no token-handing passage anywhere in the holding that states a failure at a station, which is what the first limb claims; five traditions were gone through node by node."@en ;
    skos:note "The spread, stated so that six witnesses do not read as six transmissions. The Books of Jeu and the Pistis Sophia are one family with a documented route — the second cites the first by name, twice — so the first limb rests on two transmissions and not four. The second limb rests on the Hekhalot material and on a Greek report about the Mithraic mysteries, between which no route has been proposed. Four transmissions in all, and the weakest point is that the two behind the first limb are as close as two transmissions in this holding get."@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by either of two cases. A transmission in which a station admits the passer on a token the station or the text supplies, and which nevertheless states a test there, or names a case in which somebody was refused, or states what a wrong token would cost. Or a transmission in which a station puts a question or an ordeal to the passer and the text states only the passing side, with nothing about failing it. One clean case of either kind ends the statement; the Egyptian material above is the nearest thing the holding has to the first, and it is nearest because its classification is arguable and not because its wording is."@en ;
    skos:note "Untested. No pm:Testing points here, which is the state and not an omission. What a protocol would have to settle first is the population, and here that is unusually tractable: the unit is a station in a transmission that carries a graded passage, and the ascent material of this holding is small enough to enumerate. The harder question is the one the first limb hides — whether the absence of a stated failure is a property of these transmissions or of the state of their texts, several of which break off."@en .
